 "rules_written_before_scoring": "Written down before any weighting was scored: - The projects are put in alphabetical order and every second one is held out. Settings are chosen on the others only. - Each bug is asked two ways: with its full report (title and text), and with its title alone, which stands in for   the short question a person or a coding agent usually types. - The setting chosen is the one with the highest share of bugs, averaged over the two ways of asking, for which a file   the real fix changed is among the first five returned (the benchmark's headline measure). Between equal settings:   the one nearest the search as it was (words counting more, then the smaller constant). - The search is changed only if, on the held-out projects, the chosen setting is better than the search as it was by   that same average and not more than half a point worse on either way of asking. Whatever the held-out figures are,   they are the ones reported. One thing was known beforehand and is not hidden: the first run's figures per project for meaning alone and for the equal fusion, on full reports, had been seen. The split rule is mechanical and is the one benchmark_meaning.py used.",
